    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 172035\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n135 PM MST Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Chances for light rain will heighten over Arizona this weekend \nwith the best chances for accumulations focused over higher terrain \nlocations. \n\n- Temperatures will gradually warm closer to the seasonal normal \nthrough the middle of next week.\n  \n- A Pacific low pressure system moving into the Southwest during the \nmiddle and end of next week will result in much better rainfall \nchances with the possibility of a few embedded thunderstorms, as \nwell as cooler temperatures \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Sunday/... \nPronounced Rex blocking currently over the western Conus will \ntransition into a more progressive pattern over the next 48 hours as \nstrong jet energy entering the East Pacific acts to deamplify the \nridge axis while also forcing the absorption the associated southern \nstream cutoff low into the mean westerly flow aloft. The latter of \nthese two processes will take much longer for completion resulting \nin a prolonged period of southerly moist advection through the H7-H5 \nlayer yielding total column PWATS in excess of 1\" across much of the \nCWA. This pattern configuration will also place south-central Arizona \nwithin a favorable ascent position in relation to an upper level jet \nstreak able to tap some of this thick midlevel moisture. However, \nthe sfc-H8 layer remain exceptionally dry due to persistent \ndownsloping easterly winds, and this deep sub-cloud dry layer will \nneed to be at least partially moistened before more notable chances \nof measurable rain can be achieved. Based on the synoptic ascent \nstructure, a weakening easterly low level gradient, and trends in \nregional WV imagery, feel this evolution will be more effective than \nsuggested by base NBM POPS, and have boosted the chances for light \naccumulations through Sunday morning. \n\nForecast uncertainty Sunday afternoon and evening is greater than \nusual for a 24-48 time frame with some measure of midlevel cyclonic \nflow hovering over south-central Arizona as the upper wave only \nslowly gets stretched and deamplifies. Moisture should gradually \nbecome scoured from the cloud layer as winds veer more \nsouthwesterly, but the process may be more delayed with the slow \ntransition. Thus, its not out of the question a few light showers \nlinger throughout the day, though becoming primarily focused in \norographically favored areas east of Phoenix. By Sunday evening, the \nupper low will finally begin ejecting into northern Arizona, but \neven so, moist upslope through Gila County could still support a \ncouple isolated light showers into Monday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Monday through Friday/...\nBy early next week, high pressure will be well established over NE \nCONUS, while the aforementioned wave becomes absorbed into the mean \nSW flow head of the next incoming low pressure system diving down \nthe west coast. Another low chance (20-30%) of showers may be \nmanifested Monday morning across the higher terrain of south-central \nArizona , however if anything does develop, total accumulations are \nexpected to be minimal. By Tuesday, deep troughing and strong \nnegative height anomalies will amplify and intensify along the west \ncoast, however confidence in the timing and depth of this feature \nremains low to moderate. The deterministic ECMWF forecasts a slower \nand deeper solution yielding a more robust rainfall scenario, while \nthe GFS deterministic provides a faster outcome lifting the cold \ncore into southern Utah resulting in a more tempered rainfall \npotential. Regardless, the timing of the best rainfall potential is \nhighlighting the Wednesday and Thursday time frame while pattern \nrecognition and some instability parameters suggest the potential for \nstrong thunderstorms ahead of the shortwave passage. Additionally, \nwith the uncertainty of this next system, a wide spread in NBM \ntemperatures have presented themselves throughout the week, though \nbelow normal highs are most likely. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1725Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main aviation weather concerns in this TAF package will be the \ngusty easterly winds and slantwise visibility reductions due to \nlofted dust. Winds will remain easterly through the TAF period.\nElevated winds with gusts of 20-25 kt will continue through 21Z \nbefore tapering off through the afternoon hours. The gusty winds \nwill continue to keep slantwise visibilities reduced due to lofted \ndust. Winds will decrease below 12 kt sustained this afternoon, and \nin conjunction, slantwise visibility will also improve.\n\nGuidance continues to show an increase in shower chances heading \ninto the evening through the overnight hours with VCSH across all \nterminals. Uncertainty of overall SHRA coverage still precludes a \nmention of -SHRA in the TAFs, however, any operational impacts \nshould remain minimal. VCSH will continue into tomorrow morning and \nis expected to move NE of the terminals by 15Z. Considerable cloud \ncover with bases as low as 9-12 kft will be common starting this \nafternoon, potentially lowering to as low as 7 kft this evening into \nthe overnight period in any more persistent SHRA activity. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nWinds at KIPL will be out of the NE before going light and variable \nearly this evening. Winds will also be out of the NE at KBLH before \ngoing more northerly early this evening. Wind speeds will generally \nbe aob 11 kt through the period. Considerable cloud cover with bases \nas low as 8-12 kft will continue through this evening before overall \ncoverage gradually decreases during the overnight period. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWind speeds through the beginning of next week will be much weaker \nthan the past several days with minimal gustiness. Light showers \nwill affect much of the Arizona districts this weekend, and \nparticularly over eastern districts, but the chance for wetting rain \nis low (~10%). Minimum humidity levels will improve closer to a 30-\n50% range during the first half of next week, then likely rise \nfurther during the latter half of the week. Overnight recovery \nshould be good to excellent. A Pacific weather disturbance will \nbring increased chances of wetting rainfall, breezy winds, and \ncooler temperatures during the latter half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18/Ryan \nAVIATION...Berislavich/Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...18/Ryan\n",
